There are only three relationships to check while walking from left to right. An interval can finish before the new one, start after it, or overlap it. In the second case, the finished new interval is stored and the current interval becomes the one being tracked.

This handles insertion at the beginning, middle, or end without a separate sorting step. The result is new, so the input slices are not changed.

```go
func insertNewInterval(intervals [][]int, newInterval []int) [][]int {
	result := make([][]int, 0, len(intervals)+1)
	current := []int{newInterval[0], newInterval[1]}

	for _, interval := range intervals {
		if interval[1] < current[0] {
			result = append(result, interval)
		} else if current[1] < interval[0] {
			result = append(result, current)
			current = []int{interval[0], interval[1]}
		} else {
			current[0] = minInt(current[0], interval[0])
			current[1] = maxInt(current[1], interval[1])
		}
	}

	result = append(result, current)
	return result
}

func minInt(a int, b int) int {
	if a < b {
		return a
	}
	return b
}

func maxInt(a int, b int) int {
	if a > b {
		return a
	}
	return b
}
```

A useful edge case is an empty `intervals` slice. The loop is skipped and the copied new interval is returned. Equal endpoints count as overlap here, so `[2, 4]` and `[4, 7]` become `[2, 7]`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the result.

Rather than split the work into three loops, this keeps one pending interval. Intervals that are completely before it are added immediately. When an interval is completely after it, the pending range is saved and the current interval becomes pending. Every other case is an overlap.

The important part is adding the pending range only after all possible overlaps have been checked.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public int[][] insertNewInterval(int[][] Intervals, int[] newInterval) {
        List<int[]> result = new ArrayList<>();
        int[] pending = new int[]{newInterval[0], newInterval[1]};

        for (int[] interval : Intervals) {
            if (interval[1] < pending[0]) {
                result.add(new int[]{interval[0], interval[1]});
            } else if (pending[1] < interval[0]) {
                result.add(pending);
                pending = new int[]{interval[0], interval[1]};
            } else {
                pending[0] = Math.min(pending[0], interval[0]);
                pending[1] = Math.max(pending[1], interval[1]);
            }
        }

        result.add(pending);
        return result.toArray(new int[result.size()][]);
    }
}
```

A quick dry run with `[[1, 2], [6, 9]]` and `[3, 7]` first stores `[1, 2]`, then merges `[3, 7]` with `[6, 9]` into `[3, 9]`. The final result is `[[1, 2], [3, 9]]`.

This version copies each interval into the result, so later changes to the returned array do not alter the original interval rows.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

This version treats the new range like one more interval. It copies all intervals into a list, adds the new one, sorts by start, and then performs the usual merge scan.

It is a little less efficient than using the sorted input directly, but the steps are easy to follow and it does not change `Intervals`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public int[][] insertNewInterval(int[][] Intervals, int[] newInterval) {
        List<int[]> all = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int[] interval : Intervals) {
            all.add(new int[]{interval[0], interval[1]});
        }
        all.add(new int[]{newInterval[0], newInterval[1]});

        all.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(interval -> interval[0]));

        List<int[]> merged = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int[] interval : all) {
            if (merged.isEmpty() || merged.get(merged.size() - 1)[1] < interval[0]) {
                merged.add(interval);
            } else {
                int[] last = merged.get(merged.size() - 1);
                last[1] = Math.max(last[1], interval[1]);
            }
        }

        return merged.toArray(new int[merged.size()][]);
    }
}
```

An empty input still works because the new interval is added before the merge loop. Touching ranges such as `[1, 3]` and `[3, 5]` are merged because the gap check uses `<`.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because of sorting.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

Since the existing intervals are sorted by their starting values, a binary search can find where the new interval belongs. After inserting it into a copy, one merge pass produces the final answer.

The binary search does not improve the total bound because the copied vector still has to be scanned, but it shows how the sorted property can be used to find the position.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> insertNewInterval(vector<vector<int>>& Intervals, vector<int>& newInterval){
        vector<vector<int>> ordered = Intervals;

        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(ordered.size());

        while (left < right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;
            if (ordered[middle][0] < newInterval[0]) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle;
            }
        }

        ordered.insert(ordered.begin() + left, newInterval);

        vector<vector<int>> result;
        for (const vector<int>& interval : ordered) {
            if (result.empty() || result.back()[1] < interval[0]) {
                result.push_back(interval);
            } else {
                result.back()[1] = max(result.back()[1], interval[1]);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

For example, with starts `1, 6` and a new start of `2`, the search returns position `1`. The inserted range is then merged with any neighbor it touches.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, including vector insertion and the merge scan.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the copied and returned intervals.

